nLet me recall that cos3θ = 4 cos³θ - 3 cosθ = cosθ (4 cos²θ - 3) = cosθ (4x - 3).\n\nSince x = [4 - √2]/8, so 4x - 3 = 4*[4 - √2]/8 - 3 = [4 - √2]/2 - 3 = 2 - (√2)/2 - 3 = -1 - (√2)/2.\n\nTherefore, cos3θ = cosθ * [ -1 - (√2)/2 ].\n\nBut we also have AC = -4 cos3θ. So, AC = -4 * [cosθ * (-1 - √2/2)] = 4 cosθ (1 + √2/2).\n\nBut we also have from earlier that AP = AC / (2 cosθ) = [4 cosθ (1 + √2/2)] / (2 cosθ) = 2(1 + √2/2) = 2 + √2.\n\nSo, AP = 2 + √2.\n\nSince AB = 4, then BP = AB - AP = 4 - (2 + √2) = 2 - √2.\n\nTherefore, the ratio AP / BP = (2 + √2) / (2 - √2).\n\nTo rationalize the denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (2 + √2):\n\n[(2 + √2)(2 + √2)] / [(2 - √2)(2 + √2)] = [ (4 + 4√2 + 2) ] / (4 - 2) = (6 + 4√2)/2 = 3 + 2√2.\n\nWait, but the problem says the ratio can be represented as p + q√r, where p, q, r are positive integers and r is not divisible by the square of any prime. Here, 3 + 2√2 fits, with p = 3, q = 2, r = 2. But wait, r = 2 is allowed since it\'s not divisible by the square of any prime (2 is prime, and 2² = 4 doesn\'t divide 2). So, p + q + r = 3 + 2 + 2 = 7.\n\nBut let me verify the calculations step by step to ensure no mistakes.\n\nFirst, we found x = cos²θ = [4 - √2]/8.\n\nThen, computed cos3θ = 4 cos³θ - 3 cosθ = cosθ(4 cos²θ - 3) = cosθ(4x - 3). Since x = [4 - √2]/8, 4x = [4 - √2]/2, so 4x - 3 = [4 - √2]/2 - 3 = (4 - √2 - 6)/2 = (-2 - √2)/2 = -1 - √2/2. Therefore, cos3θ = cosθ * [-1 - √2/2]. Then AC = -4 cos3θ = 4 cosθ (1 + √2/2). Then AP = AC / (2 cosθ) = 2(1 + √2/2) = 2 + √2. \n\nYes, that seems correct. Then BP = AB - AP = 4 - (2 + √2) = 2 - √2. Then AP / BP = (2 + √2)/(2 - √2) = 3 + 2√2. \n\nYes, and 3 + 2√2 is in the form p + q√r with p = 3, q = 2, r = 2. Therefore, p + q + r = 3 + 2 + 2 = 7.\n\nBut wait, the problem says "the ratio AP/BP can be represented in the form p + q√r". However, usually, ratios are positive, and here it\'s 3 + 2√2, which is positive. So the answer is 7.\n\nBut let me check once again if AP =