d 11 $. Try $ x = 4 $:  \n  $ 3 \\cdot 4 = 12 \\equiv 1 \\mod 11 $, so $ 3^{-1} \\equiv 4 \\mod 11 $\n\n- **5⁻¹ mod 11**: Try $ x = 9 $:  \n  $ 5 \\cdot 9 = 45 \\equiv 1 \\mod 11 $, so $ 5^{-1} \\equiv 9 \\mod 11 $\n\n- **7⁻¹ mod 11**: Try $ x = 8 $:  \n  $ 7 \\cdot 8 = 56 \\equiv 1 \\mod 11 $, so $ 7^{-1} \\equiv 8 \\mod 11 $\n\nThus:\n\n$$\n3^{-1} + 5^{-1} + 7^{-1} \\equiv 4 + 9 + 8 = 21 \\mod 11\n$$\n\nNow, reduce modulo 11:\n\n$$\n21 \\mod 11 = 10\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n3^{-1} + 5^{-1} + 7^{-1} \\equiv 10 \\mod 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Compute the Inverse of the Result\n\nNow we need:\n\n$$\na \\equiv (3^{-1} + 5^{-1} + 7^{-1})^{-1} \\equiv 10^{-1} \\mod 11\n$$\n\nWe now find the modular inverse of 10 modulo 11. That is, find $ x $ such that:\n\n$$\n10x \\equiv 1 \\mod 11\n$$\n\nTrying values:\n\n- $ 10 \\cdot 10 = 100 \\equiv 1 \\mod 11 $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n10^{-1} \\equiv 10 \\mod 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Step: Conclude the Value of $ a $\n\nFrom above, we have:\n\n$$\na \\equiv 10 \\mod 11\n$$\n\nThus, the remainder when $ a $ is divided by 11 is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{10}\n$$<|im_end|><|im_end|>\n'], label: 10, reward: 1.0
