from a₂, the terms are repeating every 6 terms? Wait, let me check:\n\na₂ = 1/2, a₃ = 1/4, a₄ = 3/4, a₅ = 1/2, a₆ = 1/3, a₇ = 2/3, a₈ = 1/2, a₉ = 3/8, a₁₀ = 5/8, a₁₁ = 1/2, a₁₂ = 2/5, a₁₃ = 3/5, a₁₄ = 1/2... Hmm, so from a₂ to a₈, the sequence is:\n\n1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/2, 1/3, 2/3, 1/2, 3/8, 5/8, 1/2, 2/5, 3/5, 1/2,...\n\nWait, so starting from a₂, the sequence is periodic? But the period seems to be longer than 6? Let me check.\n\nWait, if a₂ = 1/2, a₃ = 1/4, a₄ = 3/4, a₅ = 1/2, a₆ = 1/3, a₇ = 2/3, a₈ = 1/2, a₉ = 3/8, a₁₀ = 5/8, a₁₁ = 1/2, a₁₂ = 2/5, a₁₃ = 3/5, a₁₄ = 1/2,...\n\nSo after a₁₁ = 1/2, the next term is a₁₂ = 2/5, then a₁₃ = 3/5, then a₁₄ = 1/2 again. So from a₂ to a₁₄, the terms are:\n\na₂: 1/2\n\na₃: 1/4\n\na₄: 3/4\n\na₅: 1/2\n\na₆: 1/3\n\na₇: 2/3\n\na₈: 1/2\n\na₉: 3/8\n\na₁₀: 5/8\n\na₁₁: 1/2\n\na₁₂: 2/5\n\na₁₃: 3/5\n\na₁₄: 1/2\n\nSo, between a₂ and a₁₄, there's a pattern where every odd index term after a₂ is 1/2, but the even indices?\n\nWait, no. Let me check:\n\na₂ is 1/2 (even index),\n\na₃ is 1/4 (odd index),\n\na₄ is 3/4 (even index),\n\na₅ is 1/2 (odd index),\n\na₆ is 1/3 (even index),\n\na₇ is 2/3 (odd index),\n\na₈ is 1/2 (even index),\n\na₉ is 3/8 (odd index),\n\na₁₀ is 5/8 (even index),\n\na₁₁ is 1/2 (odd index),\n\na₁₂ is 2/5 (even index),\n\na₁₃ is 3/5 (odd index),\n\na₁₄ is 1/2 (even index).\n\nSo, the even indices (starting from 2) have terms:\n\na₂: 1/2\n\na₄: 3/4\n\na₆: 1/3\n\na₈: 1/2\n\na₁₀: 5/8\n\na₁₂: 2/5\n\na₁₄: 1/2\n\nAnd the odd indices (starting from 3):\n\na₃: 1/4\n\na₅: 1/2\n\na₇: 2/3\n\na₉: 3/8\n\na₁₁: 1/2\n\na₁₃: 3/5\n\nSo, the odd indices after a₃ have terms that are not consistent. Wait, a₅ is 1/2, a₇ is 2/3, a₉ is 3/8, a₁₁ is 1/2, a₁₃ is 3/5. Hmm, seems like there's a pattern here but maybe not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sequence has a period of 6? But from a₂ to a₈, the terms are:\n\n1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/2, 1/3, 2/3, 1/2...\n\nBut then a₉ is 3/8, which is different from a₃ (1/4), so that's not a period of 6.