other points where both |α + 1| = 2 and |α² + 1| = 2?\n\nSuppose α is not purely imaginary. Let me assume α has real and imaginary parts. Then we have:\n\nFrom |α + 1|² = 4 ⇒ x² + y² + 2x = 3\n\nFrom |α² + 1|² = 4 ⇒ (x² - y² + 1)^2 + (2xy)^2 = 4\n\nLet me substitute x² + y² = 3 - 2x into the second equation.\n\nFirst, let me compute x² - y²:\n\nSince x² + y² = 3 - 2x, then x² - y² = (x² + y²) - 2y² = (3 - 2x) - 2y². But this seems messy.\n\nAlternatively, let me plug in specific values. Suppose α = i√3, then it satisfies both equations.\n\nWhat if α = 0 + i√3? Then:\n\n|α + 1| = √(1 + 3) = 2, which is good.\n\nAnd |α² + 1| = |(-3) + 1| = 2, which is good.\n\nAnother idea: Let’s set α = i√3. Then α² = -3, which gives |α² - 1| = |-4| = 4, and |α - 1| = √(1 + 3) = 2. So 4 = 2 × 2, which works.\n\nSimilarly, α = -i√3: same thing. Thus, both are solutions.\n\nAre there other complex numbers?\n\nLet me suppose α = i√3, then α² = -3, so α⁴ = 9. Then |α⁴ - 1| = |9 - 1| = 8. And 4|α - 1| = 4 × 2 = 8. So this works.\n\nBut what if α = i√2? Then:\n\n|α + 1| = √(1 + 2) = √3 ≈ 1.732 ≠ 2. So it doesn't work.\n\nAnother idea: Let’s take α = e^{iθ}. Then α + 1 = e^{iθ} + 1, and |α + 1| = 2 ⇒ |e^{iθ} + 1| = 2.\n\nThis is true when e^{iθ} lies on the circle of radius 2 centered at -1. That happens when θ = π/2 or 3π/2, which gives α = i and α = -i. But then α² = -1, and |α² + 1| = |-1 + 1| = 0 ≠ 2. So that doesn't work.\n\nSo the only solutions are α = i√3 and α = -i√3.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{-i\\sqrt{3}}, \\quad \\boxed{i\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\n**Final Answer**\n$$\n\\boxed{-i\\sqrt{3}}, \\quad \\boxed{i\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we are given:\n\n- A complex number $\\alpha \\neq 1$,\n- The two distance conditions:\n  $$\n  |\\alpha^2 - 1| = 2|\\alpha - 1| \\quad \\text{(1)}\n  $$\n  $$\n  |\\alpha^4 - 1| = 4|\\alpha - 1| \\quad \\text{(2)}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Transform the Equations\n\nRecall that:\n$$\n|\\alpha^2 - 1| = |\\alpha - 1| 