o a common denominator of 35:\n\n- $ 2 = \\frac{70}{35} $\n- $ \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{21}{35} $\n- $ \\frac{1}{7} = \\frac{5}{35} $\n\nAdd these:\n\n$$\nf(2) = \\frac{\\frac{70 + 21 + 5}{35}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{96}{35}}{2} = \\frac{48}{35}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{48}{35}}\n$$<|im_end|><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nPlease review your solution carefully. If you find a mistake, correct it. Then give your final answer within \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n<think>\nOkay, let's go through the solution step by step again to make sure everything is correct.\n\nWe started with the functional equation:\n\n$$\nf(x) + f\\left( \\frac{x + 1}{1 - 3x} \\right) = x\n$$\n\nThen we defined $ T(x) = \\frac{x + 1}{1 - 3x} $ and noticed that applying $ T $ three times brings us back to the original value, forming a cycle of length 3. That is:\n\n- $ T(T(T(x))) = x $\n\nWe used this to set up three equations:\n\n1. $ f(x) + f(T(x)) = x $\n2. $ f(T(x)) + f(T(T(x))) = T(x) $\n3. $ f(T(T(x))) + f(x) = T(T(x)) $\n\nAdding all three equations gives:\n\n$$\nf(x) + f(T(x)) + f(T(T(x))) = x + T(x) + T(T(x))\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{x + T(x) + T(T(x))}{2}\n$$\n\nWait, hold on! The original equations were:\n\n- $ f(x) + f(T(x)) = x $\n- $ f(T(x)) + f(T(T(x))) = T(x) $\n- $ f(T(T(x))) + f(x) = T(T(x)) $\n\nSo adding them:\n\n$$\nf(x) + f(T(x)) + f(T(T(x))) = x + T(x) + T(T(x))\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{x + T(x) + T(T(x))}{2}\n$$\n\nBut this is only correct if the original equations were set up correctly. So the earlier step was correct.\n\nThen, we substituted $ x = 2 $:\n\n- $ T(2) = \\frac{3}{-5} = -\\frac{3}{5} $\n- $ T(- \\frac{3}{5}) = \\frac{1}{7} $\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nf(2) = \\frac{2 + \\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{1}{7}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet me re-calculate that.\n\nFirst, compute the numerator:\n\n$$\n2 - \\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nConvert to a common denominator of 35:\n\n- $ 2 = \\frac{70}{35} $\n- $