given a situation where:\n\n- A set of $ n $ participants participates in a basketball tournament.\n- Each participant may be in any number of 5-person teams, but **no two teams may have the same 5 members** (i.e., all teams are unique and distinct).\n- For all subsets of size 9, the **average number of complete 5-person teams whose members are within the subset** is equal to the reciprocal of the average over all subsets of size 8 of the same quantity.\n\nWe are to find how many values of $ n $ (with $ 9 \\leq n \\leq 2017 $) satisfy this condition.\n\n---\n\n### Mathematical Reformulation\n\nLet’s define:\n\n- For a subset of size $ k $, the number of 5-member teams that can be entirely within that subset is $ \\binom{k}{5} $.\n- Assuming that all teams are distinct (no two teams have the same 5 members), the number of such teams in any subset of size 9 is $ \\binom{9}{5} = 126 $.\n- Similarly, the number in any subset of size 8 is $ \\binom{8}{5} = 56 $.\n\nBut the problem is not simply about the count of such teams across all subsets. It\'s about the **expected** value: that is,\n\n$$\n\\text{Average number of teams in a random subset of size 9} = \\frac{C(n, 5) \\cdot C(n - 5, 4)}{C(n, 9)}\n$$\n$$\n\\text{Average number of teams in a random subset of size 8} = \\frac{C(n, 5) \\cdot C(n - 5, 3)}{C(n, 8)}\n$$\n\nWe are given:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\binom{n}{5} \\binom{n - 5}{4}}{\\binom{n}{9}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\binom{n}{5} \\binom{n - 5}{3}}{\\binom{n}{8}}}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n$$\n\\binom{n - 5}{4} \\cdot \\binom{n}{8} = \\binom{n}{9} \\cdot \\binom{n - 5}{3}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Solving the Key Equation\n\nLet’s express all terms in terms of factorials and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(n - 5)!}{4! (n - 9)!} \\cdot \\frac{n!}{8! (n - 8)!} = \\frac{n!}{9! (n - 9)!} \\cdot \\frac{(n - 5)!}{3! (n - 8)!}\n$$\n\nCanceling terms to reduce the equation, we find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(n - 8)^2}{36} = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (n - 8)^2 = 36 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad n = 14\n$