\n\nThis process is taking too long. Maybe there\'s a better way. Let me think.\n\nEarlier, we saw that n=20 is invalid because n+1=21. But what about n=24? n=24 is invalid because n+1=25 is divisible by 5. Similarly, n=19 is invalid. \n\nWait, let\'s think of the condition that 91 cannot be formed. So for n, when trying to solve 5a + nb + (n+1)c = 91, there are no non-negative integers a, b, c.\n\nMaybe instead of checking each n, we can find the constraints that n must satisfy.\n\nFrom earlier, we saw that for 91 to be formable, there must exist some combination. But we need it not to be formable.\n\nSo let\'s think of the equation 5a + nb + (n+1)c = 91.\n\nLet me rearrange this as 5a + c = 91 - n(b + c).\n\nSo, 5a + c must be equal to 91 - n(b + c). Since a, b, c are non-negative integers, the right side must also be non-negative and divisible by 5? No, because 5a + c is not necessarily divisible by 5. Wait, no: 5a + c = (something). It\'s equal to 91 - n(b + c). So, 91 - n(b + c) must be equal to 5a + c. Therefore, 91 - n(b + c) must be equal to 5a + c. So, 91 must be congruent to n(b + c) + 5a + c mod 5. But since 5a is 0 mod 5, it\'s 91 ≡ n(b + c) + c mod 5. Which is 91 ≡ c + n(b + c) mod 5.\n\nBut 91 ≡ 1 mod 5. So, 1 ≡ c + n(b + c) mod 5.\n\nWhich can be written as 1 ≡ c(1 + n) + nb mod 5.\n\nThis is complex, but maybe we can think of it as 1 ≡ c(1 + n) + nb mod 5. Since n is coprime with 5, and c is an integer, we can find possible c that satisfy this.\n\nAlternatively, for 91 to not be formable, there must be no integer c ≥ 0 such that 1 ≡ c(1 + n) + nb mod 5. But this seems too abstract.\n\nAnother idea: For 91 to not be formable, all solutions to 5a + nb + (n+1)c = 91 must have a, b, c negative or non-integer. But this is too vague.\n\nAlternatively, think of it as a Diophantine equation. Let\'s fix n and try to see if 91 can be expressed.\n\nBut without a systematic way, this is difficult.\n\nWait, maybe we can use the following approach: if n is such that 91 is not formable, then for all k > 91, there must be a combination. Since 5 and n are coprime, once you have a sufficient number of 5s, you can form any number greater than 5n - 5. So, if 5n - 5 ≤ 91, then numbers above that can be formed with 5 and n, and adding n+1 allows forming numbers beyond that. However, if 5n - 5 > 91, then numbers above 91 might still be formable with 5, n, n+1.\n\nBut since n is such that 91 is the Frobenius number, then 5n - 5 must be > 91. So, 5n - 5 > 91 ⇒ 5n > 96 ⇒ n > 19.2 ⇒ n ≥ 20. But earlier, n=20 is invalid because n+1=21 is not divisible by 5, but n=20 is divisible by 5. So n ≥ 20, but n not divisible by 5, and n+1 not divisible by 5. So n ≥ 20, n not divisible by 5, and n+1 not divisible by 5.\n\nSo n can be 21, 22, 23, 26, 27, 28, 31, 32, 33, 36, etc.\n\nSo let\'s check n=21, 22, 23, 26, 27, 28, 31, 32, 33, 36, etc.\n\nPreviously, we checked n=21, 22, 23, 26, 27, 28, and they were all formable with 91. Let\'s try n=26 again: denominations 5, 26, 27. We saw that 91 is formable with a=13, b=1, c=0. So, n=26 is out.\n\nn=27: denominations 5, 27, 28. We saw 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=28: denominations 5, 28, 29. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=31: denominations 5, 31, 32. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=32: denominations 5, 32, 33. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=33: denominations 5, 33, 34. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=36: denominations 5, 36, 37. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nHmm, all these are formable. Let\'s try n=41: denominations 5, 41, 42. Previously, 91 is formable. Out.\n\nn=42: denominations 5, 42, 43. 91 is formable. Out.\n\nWait, maybe there are no such n? But the problem says there are, and asks to find them and sum. So I must have missed something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a specific value of n where 91 is not formable. Let me try n=16 again. Wait, we saw that n=16 is formable. What about n=17? Also formable. n=18? Formable. n=19 invalid. n=20 invalid. n=21 formable. n=22 formable. n=23 formable. n=24 invalid. n=25 invalid. n=26 